    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in exile and enthroned at Kalanaur in 1556, Akbar secured the Mughal throne under his regent Bairam Khan, whom he dismissed in 1560.</li><li>By war, marriage and diplomacy he enlarged the empire to reach from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, and ordered it by ranked service and a land revenue assessed on ten-year averages.</li><li>He won the support of non-Muslim subjects through tolerance and high office, and moved from orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to debates among all faiths, against the resistance of orthodox theologians.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Akbar never learned to read or write and is believed to have been dyslexic; he was read to every day, and he created a library of over 24,000 volumes, doing much of the cataloguing himself.</p></aside>
    </section>

