    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1542</span><span class="rf-text">Akbar is born at Amarkot, where his exiled parents had been given refuge by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ruler Rana Prasad</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1556</span><span class="rf-text">He succeeds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> in February, and in November his army under Bairam Khan defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1560</span><span class="rf-text">He dismisses his regent Bairam Khan and orders him to leave on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1568</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a> falls after a siege of four months, and the defenders and 30,000 non-combatants are massacred</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1573</span><span class="rf-text">A victory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> in September completes the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1575</span><span class="rf-text">He builds the Ibadat Khana, the House of Worship, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> for religious debates</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1585</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> becomes a Mughal province, and Akbar moves his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> for thirteen years in the north</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1605</span><span class="rf-text">He falls ill with dysentery in October and dies; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a> succeeds him as Jahangir</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akbar was born Jalal-ud-Din Muhammad Akbar on 15 October 1542 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> fortress of Amarkot, in modern-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, where his parents had been given refuge by the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ruler, Rana Prasad. His father, the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>, had been defeated at Chausa in 1539 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> in 1540 by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> and had fled westward; there he married the 14-year-old Hamida Banu Begum.</p><p>During Humayun's long exile Akbar was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> by his paternal uncles Kamran Mirza and Askari Mirza and by his aunts. He spent his youth learning to hunt, run and fight; he never learned to read or write, but had someone read to him in the evenings. His childhood tutors, among them two Irani Shias, were largely above sectarian prejudice and contributed to his later inclination towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. In 1551, after his uncle Hindal Mirza died in battle against Kamran Mirza's forces, Humayun appointed the nine-year-old Akbar governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, gave him Hindal's retainers, lands and military command, and betrothed him to Hindal's daughter Ruqaiya Sultan Begum; they were married several years later, when both were about fourteen.</p><p>Humayun reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1555 with an army partly provided by his Persian ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a>, and died a few months later. Akbar's guardian, Bairam Khan, concealed the death to prepare the succession. On 14 February 1556, in the midst of a war against Sikandar Shah to reclaim the Mughal throne, Akbar was enthroned by Bairam Khan at Kalanaur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a>, "King of Kings".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bairam Khan ruled on Akbar's behalf until he came of age. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>'s death the Surs reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> emperor and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> from the Indo-Gangetic Plains. Akbar's army, led by Bairam Khan, defeated Hemu and the Sur army on 5 November 1556 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a>, and Mughal forces occupied Delhi and then Agra. Sikandar Shah Suri was beaten and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>At eighteen Akbar wished to take a more active part in affairs. Urged on by his foster mother, Maham Anga, and other relatives, he dismissed Bairam Khan after a dispute at court in the spring of 1560 and ordered him to leave on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Goaded into rebellion, Bairam Khan was defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and forgiven; he resumed his pilgrimage and was assassinated on the way by a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. In the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> that followed, Akbar's foster brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adham-khan">Adham Khan</a> kept the spoils and slaughtered the surrendered garrison with their wives and children; Akbar rode to Malwa to relieve him of command. When Adham Khan confronted him after another dispute in late 1561, the emperor threw him from a terrace into the palace courtyard at Agra, and then a second time to ensure his death.</p><p>Akbar then distributed authority among specialised ministerial posts so that no one noble should become too powerful. He put down rebellions by a clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> chiefs from 1564 and by the Mirzas, his distant cousins, and in 1566 turned back his brother Mirza Muhammad Hakim, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, who had marched into the Punjab to seize the throne.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akbar enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> to include much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Chittor, the fortress-capital of Mewar, fell in February 1568 after a siege of four months, and Ranthambore soon after; most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kings submitted, and many were bound to him by marriage alliances and rank at court. He occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> in 1572 and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> with a victory on 2 September 1573; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> was annexed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi">Battle of Tukaroi</a> in 1575. He seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in August 1581, and it became a province in 1585; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> followed, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> he took Ahmednagar Fort in August 1600 and Asirgarh on 17 January 1601.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansabdari-system">mansabdari</a> system each officer held a rank, in 33 classes, and supplied a set number of cavalry. Land revenue, after earlier methods that burdened the peasantry and bred corruption among local officials, was assessed from 1580 by the dahsala system set out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Raja Todar Mal</a>: one-third of the average produce of the previous ten years, paid in cash, with remission when harvests failed. In 1569 he founded a new capital, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>.</p><p>To keep peace in a religiously diverse empire he abolished the sectarian tax and appointed non-Muslims to high civil and military posts. In 1575 he built the Ibadat Khana, the "House of Worship", at Fatehpur Sikri, where Muslim theologians and later people of all religions debated. A declaration of 1579, signed by all the major ulema, asserted that he was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> of the age. He sought to combine the good practices of all religions in the movement known as Din-i Ilahi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the fall of Chittor Akbar had the surviving defenders and 30,000 non-combatants massacred and their heads displayed on towers throughout the region; he proclaimed the victory as that of "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> over infidels". After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> rebellion of 1573 he slew the rebel leaders and built a tower of their severed heads; several leaders of the rebel Mirzas were trampled to death under elephants. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II of Mewar</a> never submitted, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>, defeated at Haldighati in 1576, kept most of his remaining kingdom.</p><p>His religious course made enemies among the Muslim orthodoxy. In his early reign he himself suppressed sects condemned as heretical, ordering in 1567 the exhumation of a Shia buried near a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> saint in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Later the debates at the Ibadat Khana shocked orthodox theologians, who circulated rumours that he wished to forsake Islam. In 1580 a rebellion broke out in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazis</a> issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> declaring him a heretic, and in 1581 Muslim clerics rebelled to put his brother Mirza Muhammad Hakim on the throne; he suppressed both. The debates grew more acrimonious, and he ended them in 1582. The noble Aziz Koka wrote from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1594 that the discipleship Akbar promoted amounted to no more than a desire to portray his superiority in religious matters.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> his legacy is largely negative. The historian Ishtiaq Hussain Qureshi said that Akbar "had so weakened Islam through his policies that it could not be restored to its dominant position", and Pakistani historians commonly criticise his Rajput policy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1585 Akbar remained in the north for thirteen years, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, dealing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> under Abdullah Khan Shaybanid, who had seized Badakhshan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, and with the Afghan tribes of the frontier. In 1586 he negotiated a pact with Abdullah Khan; an expedition against the Afghan tribes failed, and his minister Raja Birbal was ambushed and killed at the Malandarai Pass in February 1586. Abdullah Khan died in 1598, and the last of the rebellious Afghan tribes were subdued by 1600; despite a secret hope of reconquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, Badakhshan and Balkh stayed in Uzbek hands. In 1599 he moved his capital back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, where he ruled until his death.</p><p>His last campaigns were against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultans, begun in 1593, after which he established the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a> under Prince Daniyal. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a> rebelled and then sought reconciliation, presenting his father with gold coins bearing Akbar's portrait.</p><p>On 3 October 1605 Akbar fell ill from an attack of dysentery, from which he never recovered. He is believed to have died on 26 October 1605; the date is also given as 27 October. He was buried at his mausoleum in Sikandra, Agra, a kilometre from the tomb of his favourite consort, Mariam-uz-Zamani. By the time of his death he controlled a broad sweep of territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and Badakshan. He was succeeded by Prince Salim, his first surviving son, born in 1569, later known as Jahangir. At his death there were no signs of discontent among his Muslim subjects.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akbar firmly entrenched the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after it had been threatened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> in his father's reign. Under him Mughal India developed a strong and stable economy that tripled in size and wealth, and his courts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> became centres of the arts, letters and learning, where Timurid and Perso-Islamic culture blended with Indian elements into a distinct Mughal style in painting and architecture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> became the strongest allies of the Mughals.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> kept many of his concessions, such as the ban on cow slaughter, though Jahangir did not follow his tolerance towards the Jains. Whether Din-i Ilahi was a religion is disputed: some scholars argue that the idea arose from erroneous translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak">Abul Fazl</a>'s work by British historians, and the historian M. Athar Ali wrote that sulh-e-kul, its essence, was adopted as part of general imperial administrative policy and formed the basis of Akbar's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>.</p><p>He is generally considered one of the greatest Mughal emperors. Time, citing a legacy of "pluralism and tolerance" that "underlies the values of the modern republic of India", included him in its list of top 25 world leaders. The historian Mubarak Ali has observed that in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Akbar "is conveniently ignored and not mentioned in any school textbook from class one to matriculation", and that he is criticised there for bringing Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> together as one nation, which contradicts the Two-Nation theory.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Akbar (1542–1605), the third Mughal emperor, came to the throne in 1556 and enlarged the empire across much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> by conquest, marriage alliances and diplomacy. He built a centralised administration, abolished the sectarian tax and opened his court to debate among all faiths; his conquests included the massacre at Chittor in 1568.</em></p>

